#   Comments + Keyword Clarification

#    Anton, Calculus, 2.6.46
# DESCRIPTION
# Find the derivative of a function using the chain rule

# ENDDESCRIPTION
## DBsubject(Calculus - single variable)
## DBchapter(Differentiation)
## DBsection(Chain rule (without trigonometric functions))
## Institution(John Wiley & Sons)
## Level(2)
## MO(1)
## TitleText1('Calculus')
## AuthorText1('Anton')
## EditionText1('9')
## Section1('2.6')
## Problem1('46')
## KEYWORDS('Derivative', 'Chain rule', 'tangent line')

DOCUMENT();
loadMacros('PGstandard.pl', 'PGML.pl', 'PGcourse.pl');

Context("Numeric");

$a   = 4 * random(1, 3);
$p   = random(2, 4);
$ap  = $a * $p;
$x0  = 2;
$pm1 = $p - 1;
$y0  = Compute("$a*(3/2)**$p");
$m   = Compute("$ap*(3/2)**$pm1*(5/4)");
$b   = $y0 - $m * $x0;

$f  = Formula(" $a*(x-1/x)**$p ")->reduce;
$g  = Formula(" x-1/x ");
$Df = Formula(" $ap*(x-1/x)^$pm1*(1+1/x**2)")->reduce;

$line = Formula("$m*x+$b")->reduce;

BEGIN_PGML

Find the equation of the tangent line to the graph of  [`` y(x) =[$f] ``] at [`` x =  [$x0] .``]

Equation of the tangent line is [` y = `] [_]{$line}{50}

END_PGML

BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION
Taking [`  u(x) = [$g] `] in the generalized derivative formula for [` u^[$p] `] yields

 [``` \frac{d \;\left[[$f]\right]}{dx}  = \frac{d \;\left([$a] u^[$p]\right)} {du}\frac{du}{dx}   =  [$Df]. ```]

Now, when [` x = [$x0] `], [` y =  [$y0] `] and [` y'([$x0]) =  [$m] `]

Hence, equation of tangent line is [` y = [$y0] +[$m](x-[$x0]) = [$line] `]
END_PGML_SOLUTION

ENDDOCUMENT();
